# RISC-V RV32I

## Goal

The goal of this project is to understand the RISC-V instruction set architecture (ISA), specifically the RV32I base integer instruction set.

The main objectives are to understand:

- What an ISA is
- Why RISC-V is useful
- The RV32I architecture
- RISC-V registers
- Instruction formats
- Arithmetic instructions
- Logical instructions
- Load and store instructions
- Branch instructions
- Jump instructions
- Immediate values
- The program counter
- Machine instructions
- How software is translated into instructions
- How my CPU executes RISC-V instructions

The long-term goal of the project is to use a RISC-V CPU as the processor inside the final SoC.

---

## What is an ISA?

An Instruction Set Architecture (ISA) defines the interface between software and a processor.

It specifies things such as:

- Which instructions exist
- Which registers exist
- How instructions are encoded
- How memory is accessed
- How arithmetic operations work
- How branches and jumps work
- How exceptions and interrupts are handled

The ISA can therefore be viewed as the contract between software and hardware.

Conceptually:

    Software
       │
       │ RISC-V instructions
       ▼
    ┌───────────────┐
    │   RISC-V CPU  │
    └───────────────┘
       │
       ▼
    Hardware

A compiler can generate RISC-V machine code without needing to know the exact internal implementation of the CPU.

This is one of the important advantages of an ISA.

---

## What is RISC-V?

RISC-V is an open Instruction Set Architecture.

Unlike many traditional processor architectures, the RISC-V ISA is openly specified and can be implemented without requiring a proprietary CPU architecture license.

RISC-V is designed around a modular instruction set.

Instead of defining one enormous instruction set, RISC-V defines a base ISA and optional extensions.

For example:

    RV32I
      +
    M
      +
    A
      +
    F
      +
    D
      +
    C
      ...

This allows different processors to implement different combinations of functionality.

---

## Why RISC-V?

RISC-V is particularly suitable for this project because it is:

- Open
- Well documented
- Modular
- Designed for hardware implementations
- Supported by modern compilers and development tools
- Suitable for FPGA development
- Suitable for custom SoCs
- Available in both 32-bit and 64-bit variants

It also provides a realistic architecture for learning how a modern processor works.

The final computer can therefore use a real standardized ISA rather than a completely custom instruction set.

---

## Why RV32I?

The CPU used in this project is based on RV32I.

RV32 means:

    32-bit registers
    32-bit address space

The I means:

    Base Integer Instruction Set

RV32I is the basic integer instruction set of RISC-V.

It contains the fundamental instructions needed for a general-purpose processor.

Examples include:

- "ADD"
- "SUB"
- "AND"
- "OR"
- "XOR"
- "SLT"
- "ADDI"
- "LW"
- "SW"
- "BEQ"
- "BNE"
- "JAL"
- "JALR"

RV32I does not include hardware multiplication or division.

Those are provided by the optional "M" extension.

---

## RV32I Architecture

The basic architecture is:

    ┌──────────────────────────────┐
    │          RISC-V CPU          │
    │                              │
    │  Program Counter             │
    │  Register File               │
    │  ALU                         │
    │  Control Unit                │
    │  Instruction Decoder         │
    │  Memory Interface            │
    └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                   │
                   ▼
                 Memory

The CPU fetches instructions from memory, decodes them and executes them.

The basic instruction cycle is:

    Fetch
      ↓
    Decode
      ↓
    Execute
      ↓
    Memory Access
      ↓
    Write Back
      ↓
    Fetch next instruction

A simple implementation may combine or simplify some of these stages.

---

## RISC-V Registers

RV32I contains 32 general-purpose integer registers.

They are:

    x0
    x1
    x2
    ...
    x31

Each register is:

    32 bits

Therefore the register file contains:

    32 × 32-bit registers

---

## Register x0

One of the most important RISC-V registers is:

    x0

"x0" is permanently hardwired to zero.

Therefore:

    x0 = 0

A write to "x0" has no effect.

For example:

    ADD x0, x1, x2

does not change "x0".

This is useful because many operations can use "x0" to represent a constant zero.

---

## ABI Register Names

RISC-V also provides conventional ABI names for the registers.

For example:

| Register  | ABI Name | Common Purpose                     |
|-----------|----------|------------------------------------|
| "x0"      | "zero"   | Constant zero                      |
| "x1"      | "ra"     | Return address                     |
| "x2"      | "sp"     | Stack pointer                      |
| "x3"      | "gp"     | Global pointer                     |
| "x4"      | "tp"     | Thread pointer                     |
| "x5-x7"   | "t0-t2"  | Temporaries                        |
| "x8-x9"   | "s0-s1"  | Saved registers                    |
| "x10-x17" | "a0-a7"  | Function arguments / return values |
| "x18-x27" | "s2-s11" | Saved registers                    |
| "x28-x31" | "t3-t6"  | Temporaries                        |

The hardware still uses the actual register numbers.

The ABI names are conventions used by software.

---

## Program Counter

The CPU has a Program Counter (PC).

The PC contains the address of the current instruction.

For normal sequential execution:

    PC = PC + 4

because standard RV32I instructions are 32 bits:

    32 bits = 4 bytes

For example:

    PC = 0x00001000

After executing a normal instruction:

    PC = 0x00001004

The PC changes differently for branches and jumps.

---

## Instruction Size

Base RV32I instructions are normally:

    32 bits

or:

    4 bytes

For example:

| Address      | Instruction |
|--------------|-------------|   
| 0x00001000   | ADD         |
| 0x00001004   | SUB         |
| 0x00001008   | AND         |
| 0x0000100C   | OR          |

This makes instruction fetching relatively straightforward.

The optional "C" extension introduces compressed 16-bit instructions, but this CPU does not implement that extension.

---

## RISC-V Instruction Formats

RV32I uses several instruction formats.

The main formats are:

- R-type
- I-type
- S-type
- B-type
- U-type
- J-type

These formats define how the 32 bits of an instruction are divided into fields.

---

## R-Type

R-type instructions are commonly used for register-to-register operations.

Example:

    ADD x5, x6, x7

The instruction contains:

- funct7
- rs2
- rs1
- funct3
- rd
- opcode

Conceptually:

    ┌────────┬───────┬───────┬───────┬───────┬────────┐
    │ funct7 │  rs2  │  rs1  │ funct3│  rd   │ opcode │
    │ 31..25 │ 24..20│ 19..15│ 14..12│ 11..7 │  6..0  │
    └────────┴───────┴───────┴───────┴───────┴────────┘

The CPU's instruction decoder uses these fields to determine which operation should be performed.

---

## I-Type

I-type instructions contain an immediate value.

Examples include:

- ADDI
- ANDI
- ORI
- XORI
- LW
- JALR

The general structure is:

    ┌─────────────┬───────┬───────┬───────┬────────┐
    │  immediate  │  rs1  │ funct3│  rd   │ opcode │
    │   31..20    │19..15 │14..12 │ 11..7 │  6..0  │
    └─────────────┴───────┴───────┴───────┴────────┘

The immediate is sign-extended before being used by the CPU.

---

## S-Type

S-type instructions are used for stores.

Example:

    SW x5, 8(x6)

The immediate is split across two parts of the instruction.

Conceptually:

    ┌───────────┬───────┬───────┬────────┬──────────┬────────┐
    │ imm[11:5] │ rs2   │ rs1   │ funct3 │ imm[4:0] │ opcode │
    └───────────┴───────┴───────┴────────┴──────────┴────────┘

This allows the CPU to calculate the memory address and store a register value into memory.

---

## B-Type

B-type instructions are used for conditional branches.

Examples:

- BEQ
- BNE
- BLT
- BGE
- BLTU
- BGEU

For example:

    BEQ x5, x6, label

The CPU compares two registers.

If the condition is true, the PC changes to the branch target.

Otherwise:

    PC = PC + 4

---

## U-Type

U-type instructions contain a large immediate value.

The main RV32I U-type instructions are:

- LUI
- AUIPC

These are useful for constructing larger constants and addresses.

---

## J-Type

J-type is used by:

- JAL

Example:

    JAL x1, function

The CPU jumps to another location while storing a return address in the destination register.

This is important for function calls.

---

## RISC-V Instruction Categories

The RV32I instructions implemented by the CPU can be grouped into several categories.

### Arithmetic

- "ADD"
- "SUB"
- "ADDI"

### Logical

- "AND"
- "OR"
- "XOR"
- "ANDI"
- "ORI"
- "XORI"

### Comparison

- "SLT"
- "SLTU"
- "SLTI"
- "SLTIU"

### Shifts

- "SLL"
- "SRL"
- "SRA"
- "SLLI"
- "SRLI"
- "SRAI"

### Loads

- "LB"
- "LH"
- "LW"
- "LBU"
- "LHU"

### Stores

- "SB"
- "SH"
- "SW"

### Branches

- "BEQ"
- "BNE"
- "BLT"
- "BGE"
- "BLTU"
- "BGEU"

### Jumps

- "JAL"
- "JALR"

### Upper Immediate

- "LUI"
- "AUIPC"

### System

RV32I also defines system instructions such as:

- "ECALL"
- "EBREAK"

These become particularly important when implementing operating-system support.

---

## Arithmetic Example

Consider:

    ADD x5, x6, x7

The CPU performs:

    x5 = x6 + x7

If:

    x6 = 10
    x7 = 20

then:

    x5 = 30

The data path is approximately:

    x6 ──┐
         ├──► ALU ───► x5
    x7 ──┘

---

## Immediate Example

Consider:

    ADDI x5, x6, 10

The CPU performs:

    x5 = x6 + 10

The immediate value is stored inside the instruction.

The data path becomes:

    x6 ─────────┐
                ├──► ALU ───► x5
    immediate ──┘

---

## Load Example

Consider:

    LW x5, 0(x6)

The CPU calculates:

    address = x6 + 0

and loads a 32-bit value from memory:

    memory[address] → x5

The basic path is:

    Register File
         │
         ▼
        ALU
         │
         ▼
      Address
         │
         ▼
       Memory
         │
         ▼
    Register File

---

## Store Example

Consider:

    SW x5, 0(x6)

The CPU calculates:

    address = x6 + 0

and stores:

    x5 → memory[address]

The data path is:

    x6 ──► ALU ──► Memory Address
                       ▲
                       │
                      x5

---

## Branch Example

Consider:

    BEQ x5, x6, label

The CPU compares:

    x5 == x6

If true:

    PC = branch target

If false:

    PC = PC + 4

This means that branches change the normal sequential execution of the program.

---

## Jump Example

Consider:

    JAL x1, function

The CPU:

1. Saves a return address in "x1"
2. Calculates the target address
3. Updates the PC

This allows software to call functions.

---

## Immediate Values

RISC-V instructions frequently contain immediate values.

For example:

    ADDI x5, x6, 100

The value:

    100

is part of the instruction.

The CPU's immediate generator extracts the value and sign-extends it to 32 bits.

For example:

    12-bit immediate
           ↓
    Sign extension
           ↓
    32-bit value

This is an important part of the instruction decoding logic.

---

## Sign Extension

Signed immediate values need to be sign-extended.

For example, a negative 12-bit value must become a negative 32-bit value.

Conceptually:

    12-bit:
    1111 1111 1111

becomes:

    32-bit:
    11111111111111111111111111111111

This allows the ALU to correctly perform signed arithmetic.

---

## Instruction Decoder

The instruction decoder determines what operation the CPU should perform.

For example:

    Instruction
         │
         ▼
    Instruction Decoder
         │
         ├── opcode
         ├── funct3
         ├── funct7
         ├── rs1
         ├── rs2
         └── rd

The decoder generates control signals for the rest of the CPU.

Conceptually:

    Instruction
         ↓
    Decoder
         ↓
    Control Signals
         ↓
    ALU / Registers / Memory / PC

---

## RISC-V and the ALU

The ALU implemented earlier in the project can be used as part of the RISC-V CPU.

For example:

    ADD
    SUB
    AND
    OR
    XOR
    SLT

can be implemented using ALU operations.

The RISC-V instruction decoder determines which ALU operation should be selected.

Therefore:

    RISC-V instruction
          ↓
    Instruction Decoder
          ↓
    ALU Control
          ↓
         ALU
          ↓
        Result

---

## RISC-V and the Register File

The Register File created earlier is also used by the RISC-V CPU.

The CPU reads:

    rs1
    rs2

and writes:

    rd

For example:

    ADD x5, x6, x7

means:

    Read x6
    Read x7
    Add them
    Write result to x5

The Register File therefore forms a central part of the CPU datapath.

---

## RISC-V and RAM

The RAM created earlier can eventually be used as the CPU's memory.

The CPU performs:

    Load:
    Memory → Register

and:

    Store:
    Register → Memory

This creates the basic relationship:

    RISC-V CPU
         │
         ▼
        RAM

Later, the same concept will be expanded into a complete memory system.

---

## RISC-V and the SoC

The final goal is not just to build a RISC-V CPU.

The CPU will become the central component of the SoC.

The architecture will eventually look like:

                       RISC-V CPU
                           │
                           ▼
                      System Bus
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             │             │             │
             ▼             ▼             ▼
            RAM           ROM        Peripherals
                                        │
                         ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
                         │              │              │
                        UART           GPIO           Timer
                                                       │
                                                       ▼
                                              Interrupt Controller
                                                       │
                                                       ▼
